<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <RootNamespace>OpenVSA.Ui</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyTitle>OpenVSA.Ui</AssemblyTitle>
    <AssemblyName>OpenVSA</AssemblyName>
    <Description>L5 WPF shell: docking layout, trace windows, the three-zone plot surface and the hot-spot in-place editing model.</Description>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    REQ-HAL-002: capability-driven UI. This project must contain NO instrument-specific
    conditionals and therefore does NOT reference OpenVSA.Hal.Visa. A code search for
    instrument model names in this assembly must return no matches.
  -->
  <!--
    Syncfusion WPF, for the generic shell furniture only: docking, grids, editors, ribbon.
    Trace geometry is NOT drawn by a chart control - REQ-NFR-006's decimation and REQ-UI-042's
    hot-spot framework have to be ours regardless, which leaves a chart control very little to
    contribute, and the software rasteriser already verifies REQ-UI-010 headlessly. See
    DEPENDENCIES.md for the decision and the contributor key-registration step.
  -->
  <!--
    System.Configuration for the appSettings fallback in SyncfusionLicense, which reads the key
    that App.config merges in from the git-ignored local.secrets.config.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Reference Include="System.Configuration" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The example secrets file is copied so a contributor finds it beside the executable, next to
    where the real one belongs. The real local.secrets.config is git-ignored and never copied by
    the build - it is placed by hand.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="local.secrets.config.example" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />

    <!--
      App.config's file attribute resolves relative to the running executable's config file, so
      the real secrets file has to reach the output directory too. Guarded by Exists: it is
      git-ignored and absent in a fresh clone, and an unconditional item for a missing file is a
      build warning on every build.
    -->
    <None Include="local.secrets.config"
          Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)local.secrets.config')"
          CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    REQ-NFR-002 is not the only thing that must never reach source control. The Syncfusion key is
    a credential and this repository is public, so it is INJECTED BY THE BUILD rather than
    committed: this target writes it into a generated file under obj\, which .gitignore already
    excludes, and the file is never added to the tree.

    Off unless asked for. A developer build embeds nothing and resolves the key at run time from
    the environment or local.secrets.config, exactly as before; only a release or installer build
    passes /p:EmbedSyncfusionLicenseKey=true, and only that build needs the key present.
    That is what makes Syncfusion a dev and build requirement rather than an end-user one.

    A build that asks to embed and finds no key is a WARNING and not an error, so a fork with no
    Syncfusion account can still produce an installer. It produces one that shows the Syncfusion
    evaluation banner, and the warning says so.

    THE KEY IS RECOVERABLE FROM ANY BINARY BUILT THIS WAY, AND THAT IS ACCEPTED DELIBERATELY.
    Measured, not assumed: the generated constant lands in the assembly's user string heap as an
    88 character UTF-16 literal, and it was extracted from the OpenVSA.exe inside the published
    v0.1.0 MSI with nothing more than a regular expression over the file's bytes. Keeping the key
    out of git does not keep it out of what git's releases hand people.

    Tony decided on 2026-08-22 to keep embedding anyway, and the reasoning is Syncfusion's own
    distribution model: a shipped application is MEANT to carry a registered key, which is how any
    commercial product built on these controls reaches its users. What the class remarks in
    SyncfusionLicense.cs guard against is a key sitting in the SOURCE, where it would be copied by
    anyone reading the repository and reused for their own development. A compiled binary is a
    weaker exposure than that, and the property bought with it is real: an end user needs no
    Syncfusion account and sees no evaluation banner.

    So this is a decision, not an oversight. If you are reading this because you found the key in a
    shipped binary: yes, it is there on purpose. Reopen the question only if the licence terms
    change or the account moves off a distribution licence, and take it to Tony rather than
    quietly switching the flag off, because turning it off puts an evaluation banner in front of
    every user.
  -->
  <Target Name="GenerateSyncfusionLicenseKey" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile">
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_SyncfusionKey Condition="'$(EmbedSyncfusionLicenseKey)' == 'true'">$(SYNCFUSION_LICENSE_KEY)</_SyncfusionKey>
      <_SyncfusionKeyFile>$(IntermediateOutputPath)SyncfusionEmbeddedKey.g.cs</_SyncfusionKeyFile>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <Warning Condition="'$(EmbedSyncfusionLicenseKey)' == 'true' and '$(_SyncfusionKey)' == ''"
             Text="EmbedSyncfusionLicenseKey was requested but SYNCFUSION_LICENSE_KEY is not set in the build environment. The build will produce a binary that shows the Syncfusion evaluation banner." />

    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(_SyncfusionKeyFile)"
                      Lines="// Generated by GenerateSyncfusionLicenseKey. Never commit this file.;namespace OpenVSA.Ui { internal static class SyncfusionEmbeddedKey { internal const string Value = &quot;$(_SyncfusionKey)&quot;%3B } }"
                      Overwrite="true"
                      WriteOnlyWhenDifferent="true" />

    <ItemGroup>
      <Compile Include="$(_SyncfusionKeyFile)" />
      <FileWrites Include="$(_SyncfusionKeyFile)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Syncfusion.Licensing" Version="34.1.32" />
    <PackageReference Include="Syncfusion.Tools.WPF" Version="34.1.32" />
    <PackageReference Include="Syncfusion.SfGrid.WPF" Version="34.1.32" />
    <PackageReference Include="Syncfusion.Shared.WPF" Version="34.1.32" />
    <PackageReference Include="Syncfusion.SfSkinManager.WPF" Version="34.1.32" />
    <PackageReference Include="Syncfusion.Themes.FluentDark.WPF" Version="34.1.32" />
    <PackageReference Include="Syncfusion.Themes.FluentLight.WPF" Version="34.1.32" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.Core\OpenVSA.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.Hal\OpenVSA.Hal.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.Dsp\OpenVSA.Dsp.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.Capture\OpenVSA.Capture.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.Demod\OpenVSA.Demod.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.Measurement\OpenVSA.Measurement.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.Personality\OpenVSA.Personality.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.Api\OpenVSA.Api.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Front ends are plug-ins, not references. REQ-ARC-001 bars every layer from L3 upward - this
    one included - from a compile-time reference to any L0 transport assembly, so the shell
    discovers them at run time through FrontEndRegistry instead.

    ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" is what makes that real: it sequences the build so the
    transports are compiled before this project, without putting them on the compile-time
    reference list. OpenVSA.Architecture.Tests asserts the absence, so removing this attribute
    fails the suite rather than quietly re-coupling the layers.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.Hal.Sim\OpenVSA.Hal.Sim.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" Private="false" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.Hal.File\OpenVSA.Hal.File.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" Private="false" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.Hal.Visa\OpenVSA.Hal.Visa.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" Private="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" also stops the outputs being copied, so they are copied
    explicitly into the FrontEnds subdirectory that FrontEndRegistry probes. The subdirectory is
    deliberate: it makes the plug-in boundary visible on disk, and mirrors the Personalities\
    convention of REQ-ARC-003.
  -->
  <Target Name="CopyFrontEndPlugins" AfterTargets="Build">
    <ItemGroup>
      <FrontEndPlugin Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\OpenVSA.Hal.Sim\$(OutputPath)OpenVSA.Hal.Sim.dll" />
      <FrontEndPlugin Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\OpenVSA.Hal.Sim\$(OutputPath)OpenVSA.Hal.Sim.pdb" />
      <FrontEndPlugin Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\OpenVSA.Hal.File\$(OutputPath)OpenVSA.Hal.File.dll" />
      <FrontEndPlugin Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\OpenVSA.Hal.File\$(OutputPath)OpenVSA.Hal.File.pdb" />
      <FrontEndPlugin Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\OpenVSA.Hal.Visa\$(OutputPath)OpenVSA.Hal.Visa.dll" />
      <FrontEndPlugin Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\OpenVSA.Hal.Visa\$(OutputPath)OpenVSA.Hal.Visa.pdb" />

      <!--
        Ivi.Visa is deliberately NOT copied. The IviFoundation.Visa package supplies only a
        reference assembly for .NET Framework; the implementation is installed into the GAC by the
        VISA Shared Components that ship with NI-VISA and Keysight IO Libraries alike. So the VISA
        plug-in resolves it at run time on a machine that has VISA, and fails to load on one that
        does not - which is precisely the degradation REQ-NFR-032 asks for, reported by the
        registry as an unavailable source rather than as a startup failure.
      -->
    </ItemGroup>
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(FrontEndPlugin)"
          DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)FrontEnds"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true"
          Condition="Exists('%(FrontEndPlugin.FullPath)')" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    The cross-validation harness, for issue #393's test signal source panel. Same arrangement as
    the front-end plug-ins above and for one more reason on top of theirs: the harness is TEST
    INFRASTRUCTURE, and REQ-ARC-001 bars it from becoming a dependency of the product. It is also
    the assembly that references OpenVSA.Hal.Visa, so a compile-time reference here would put VISA
    on the start-up path and break REQ-NFR-032.

    ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" gives build ordering without a reference; StimulusRegistry
    finds it at run time and degrades to a disabled menu item with a stated reason when it is not
    there.

    NOT THERE IS THE NORMAL CASE FOR AN INSTALLED COPY, and that is deliberate rather than an
    accident of packaging. The installer harvests the payload root and the FrontEnds folder by
    name; this subdirectory is in neither, so a shipped OpenVSA carries no bench harness. Driving
    a generator is equipment for cross-validating this product, not a feature of it.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.TestHarness\OpenVSA.TestHarness.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" Private="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <Target Name="CopyStimulusHarness" AfterTargets="Build">
    <ItemGroup>
      <StimulusHarnessFile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\OpenVSA.TestHarness\$(OutputPath)OpenVSA.TestHarness.dll" />
      <StimulusHarnessFile Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\OpenVSA.TestHarness\$(OutputPath)OpenVSA.TestHarness.pdb" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
      The harness alone. Its dependencies - Core, Hal, Dsp, Capture, Measurement - are already
      beside the shell, and OpenVSA.Hal.Visa is loaded from FrontEnds by the front-end registry, so
      copying a second one here would give the process two assemblies of the same identity from
      two paths. One copy, resolved the way every other plug-in here is resolved.
    -->
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(StimulusHarnessFile)"
          DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)TestHarness"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true"
          Condition="Exists('%(StimulusHarnessFile.FullPath)')" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    REQ-HAL-002's second clause is "switching front ends visibly re-ranges the affected controls",
    which is a statement about what the settings pane shows and cannot be asserted from outside the
    shell. RangeSettingsFor is internal rather than public for that: the test needs it, no caller
    outside this assembly does, and making it public to test it would put a UI implementation
    detail in the product's surface.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AssemblyAttribute Include="System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InternalsVisibleTo">
      <_Parameter1>OpenVSA.Ui.Tests</_Parameter1>
    </AssemblyAttribute>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
